"""Original concise lessons. Columns: title, principle, example, task, answer, takeaway."""
CHAPTERS=[]
def add(name,raw):
    rows=[]
    for line in raw.strip().splitlines():
        fields=line.split('|')
        assert len(fields)==6,(name,len(fields),line)
        rows.append(dict(zip(['title','principle','example','task','answer','takeaway'],fields)))
    assert len(rows)==20,(name,len(rows))
    CHAPTERS.append((name,rows))

add('01-foundations',r'''
Read the board before choosing a move|A position is a set of relationships, not a collection of pieces. Start with whose turn it is, king safety, material, and direct threats. A beautiful plan is irrelevant if a queen is hanging now.|Suppose your opponent moves a bishop onto the same diagonal as your queen. Before continuing a pawn attack, trace that diagonal and identify every blocker. The bishop may be attacking the queen immediately or preparing a discovered attack.|Name four things to check after an unfamiliar opponent move.|Check direct checks, captures, threats, and newly opened lines. Then compare the resulting position with your plan. You need not abandon the plan unless the position gives a concrete reason.|Ask what changed before asking what you want.
Coordinates make analysis precise|Files run a through h and ranks run 1 through 8. A square's name never changes when the board is viewed from Black's side. Accurate coordinates let you reconstruct lessons without relying on a picture.|White's king begins on e1 and Black's on e8. From White's side a1 is the left corner; it is a dark square. A knight described as moving from g1 to f3 has the same move in either board orientation.|Which squares lie on the a1-h8 diagonal?|a1, b2, c3, d4, e5, f6, g7, and h8. All are dark squares. This diagonal is useful for noticing a bishop or queen aiming at a king from far away.|Say the square names while replaying a line.
Read algebraic notation|K, Q, R, B, and N name pieces; a pawn move has no piece letter. The destination is always named. The x marks a capture, + a check, and # a checkmate. Move numbers identify whose turn comes next.|After 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3, White's knight has developed to f3. The notation 2...Nc6 is Black's reply on move two. A move such as exd5 means a pawn from the e-file captures onto d5; it does not specify the starting rank explicitly.|What information does Rxe7+ provide?|A rook captures something on e7 and gives check. The notation alone does not identify the captured piece. Reconstruct the board to learn that and to distinguish between two rooks that might reach e7.|Translate notation into board changes, not just sounds.
The knight's special geometry|A knight changes one coordinate by two and the other by one. It jumps over intervening pieces but cannot land on a friendly piece. It changes square color on every move, which helps you check a visualized route.|An unobstructed knight on d4 reaches b3, b5, c2, c6, e2, e6, f3, and f5. Notice that all eight destinations have the opposite color from d4. A knight on a1 has only b3 and c2.|Can a knight on d4 attack a queen on f5 despite a pawn on e4?|Yes. The pawn does not block a knight jump. Whether capturing the queen is legal still depends on whether the knight is pinned to its king.|For knight threats, check landing squares rather than lines.
Bishops need diagonals|A bishop stays on one color throughout the game. It can cross an empty diagonal quickly but cannot jump a blocker. Its value depends on which important squares its color can reach and whether its own pawns obstruct the route.|A bishop on c1 initially faces its own pawns on b2 and d2. Moving the d-pawn can open the c1-h6 diagonal. Moving an unrelated rook pawn does not solve that bishop's development problem.|Why can a bishop on c1 never capture a piece on c2?|Those squares have different colors. No sequence of bishop moves changes the bishop's color. Another piece must handle threats on the other color complex.|A bishop plan begins by tracing its usable diagonals.
Rooks need entry squares|Rooks move along ranks and files. An open file matters when it leads to a useful target or penetration square. Owning a file with no safe entry can be less valuable than defending a weakness on another file.|Imagine the d-file is open but d7 and d8 are heavily controlled. Doubling rooks there may look active yet achieve little. A preparatory move that removes a defender of d7 can turn file control into a real invasion.|What should you identify before doubling rooks on an open file?|Name an entry square, the opponent's defenders of it, and the target after entry. If none exists, compare the file operation with improving your worst piece.|File control is a means; penetration is the purpose.
Use the queen without losing time|The queen combines rook and bishop movement but is too valuable to expose casually. Early queen adventures let cheaper pieces attack her while developing. A queen move earns its place when it creates a concrete threat, wins material, or supports a necessary defense.|In 1.e4 e5 2.Qh5 Nc6, Black develops and defends e5. White must still bring out minor pieces and secure the king. A threat against f7 does not automatically justify repeating queen moves while the rest of the army sleeps.|Is every early queen move a mistake?|No. Some are sound theory or concrete tactics. The useful question is whether the queen can be attacked with gain of time and whether your undeveloped pieces can join the action.|Judge queen activity together with development and safety.
Pawn movement is irreversible|Pawns move forward but capture diagonally. They cannot retreat. A pawn move therefore changes both the squares it controls and the squares it can no longer defend. Even a harmless-looking advance creates permanent consequences.|White's pawn on g2 controls f3 and h3. After g2-g4 it controls f5 and h5 instead. The gain of space comes with a change to the king's shelter, especially if the king is on g1.|What two questions belong before a pawn push near your king?|Which useful squares will the pawn control, and which squares or lines will it leave weak? Then check immediate tactics. The balance depends on the whole position, not on a ban against pawn moves.|Every pawn push buys something and gives something away.
The king cannot enter attack|The king moves one square in any direction but may never move into check. You also cannot make another move that leaves your king attacked. This restriction overrides material gain, attacking ideas, and ordinary piece mobility.|If a rook shields its king from an enemy rook on the same file, moving that rook sideways may expose check. The sideways move is illegal unless it also captures or blocks the checking line in another way.|Can you capture the enemy king to finish a game?|No. Checkmate ends the game before a king could be captured. A legal position never permits a move that leaves your own king in check.|Legality comes before evaluation.
Answer check in three ways|When checked, examine king moves, captures of the checking piece, and blocks of a checking line. A knight check cannot be blocked. In a double check, the king must move because one ordinary capture or block cannot resolve both attacks.|A bishop checking along a diagonal might be met by interposing a piece. A knight checking from f7 cannot be stopped by placing something between it and the king. The geometry determines which defensive methods are available.|Why should you inspect captures before automatically moving the king?|Capturing the checker may retain castling rights or improve the position. But the capturing move must be legal and leave the king safe from every other attacker.|List legal defenses before choosing the most attractive one.
Checkmate is about every escape|Checkmate requires the king to be in check with no legal way to remove the attack. A dramatic checking move is insufficient if the defender can capture the checker, block, or escape. Test those possibilities explicitly.|A queen may stand next to the king and give check, yet be capturable if unsupported. The same queen placement becomes mate when a friendly piece protects her and all king escapes are covered or blocked.|What are the three tests after a candidate mating check?|Can the king move safely, can the checker be captured, and can the attack be blocked? If every legal answer fails, it is mate. For a knight check, the block test is unnecessary.|Prove mate by exhausting defenses.
Stalemate is different from mate|A player with no legal move is stalemated if the king is not in check. In standard chess this ends in a draw. Large material advantages do not change that rule, so careless checking-piece placement can throw away a win.|With Black's king on a8, White's king on c6, and White's queen on b6, Black to move has no legal move and is not checked. The a7, b7, and b8 escape squares are controlled.|Why is the sample position a draw despite White's queen?|Black cannot move the king anywhere safely, but a8 itself is not attacked. There are no other black pieces to move. Those two facts distinguish stalemate from checkmate.|When the opponent has few moves, check for stalemate before moving.
Castling has safety conditions|Castling develops a rook and relocates the king in one move. The king and chosen rook must not have moved, the squares between them must be empty, and the king cannot castle out of, through, or into check.|For White to castle kingside, e1, f1, and g1 must satisfy the king-safety tests. The rook may be attacked without preventing castling. On the queenside, b1 must be empty, but an attack on b1 alone does not forbid castling.|Can White castle through an attacked f1 square if g1 is safe?|No. The king's transit square matters. Castling is not a way to pass through check, even when the final square looks sheltered.|Prepare castling by clearing pieces and checking the king's entire route.
En passant changes two lines|An en passant capture is available immediately after an opposing pawn advances two squares and lands beside your pawn on its fifth rank. Your pawn captures as though the opposing pawn advanced one square. Waiting one turn loses that opportunity.|White pawn e5, Black pawn d7: after ...d5, White may play exd6 en passant, removing the pawn from d5. Both the e-file and fifth rank can change because two pawns leave their previous squares.|Can an en passant capture be illegal even when the pawn pattern fits?|Yes. If removing those pawns exposes your king to a rook, bishop, or queen, the capture is illegal. Apply the same king-safety test as for every other move.|Remove the captured pawn mentally before evaluating en passant.
Promotion is a choice|A pawn reaching the last rank must become a queen, rook, bishop, or knight of its own color. Promotion is part of that move. A queen is usually strongest, but a knight can give a unique check or fork, and a smaller piece can sometimes avoid stalemate.|A pawn on c7 that advances to c8 may promote even if its original queen remains on the board. There is no rule limiting a side to one queen. The position determines which promotion works best.|Why should you inspect knight promotion before automatically choosing a queen?|A knight attacks squares a queen does not from the same square. A checking promotion can gain a decisive tempo. Also compare stalemate and the opponent's immediate threats.|Promotion deserves calculation, not autopilot.
Material is a starting estimate|The familiar values pawn 1, knight 3, bishop 3, rook 5, queen 9 estimate exchange outcomes. They are not rules about who wins. King safety, activity, pawn structure, and forcing threats can outweigh the arithmetic.|Trading a rook for a bishop loses roughly two units if nothing else changes. If that trade removes the last defender of the enemy king and produces forced mate, the arithmetic becomes irrelevant.|What must you add to a material count before deciding an exchange?|Check the resulting king safety, piece activity, pawn structure, and tactical threats. Count only after including all likely recaptures, not just the first capture.|Count pieces, then evaluate the position they create.
Find undefended pieces|An undefended piece has no friendly protector on its square. It may be perfectly safe now but become a tactical target when checks or forks arrive. Several loose pieces make double attacks especially dangerous.|If a queen and rook are both loose, a knight fork can threaten both even when neither was attacked a move earlier. Moving one piece to a defended square may remove the combination without addressing the knight directly.|Does a defended piece automatically resist a tactic?|No. Its defender may be pinned, overloaded, removable, or unable to recapture because of mate. Defense must be tested through legal moves, not just counted as a line on the board.|Loose pieces deserve attention before an attack starts.
Distinguish a threat from a wish|A threat is a concrete action the opponent can carry out next if you do nothing. An attractive attacking arrangement is not necessarily a threat. Identifying the actual next move prevents both panic and neglect.|An enemy rook on an open file may look menacing. Ask where it can enter and what it would win. If every entry square is defended, you may have time for your own plan; if mate is threatened, you must react immediately.|How do you test whether your opponent's last move created a threat?|Temporarily imagine making a harmless move. Find the opponent's strongest check, capture, or direct threat afterward. Then determine whether your real candidate prevents or outweighs it.|Name the threatened move and its consequence.
The game changes phase gradually|Opening, middlegame, and endgame describe changing priorities rather than rigid move numbers. Fewer queens and heavy pieces often permit king activity. Development still matters in an early queen trade, while king safety can remain urgent in a sparse position.|After queens come off on move eight, a king may help defend central pawns, but a remaining rook can still deliver dangerous checks. Do not activate the king solely because someone called the position an endgame.|What practical sign suggests the king can become an active piece?|The opponent has limited checking power and you can identify safe central squares. Verify the route tactically before moving. Piece count is a clue, not permission.|Update your priorities as the available threats change.
Build a complete move habit|A reliable move habit combines an opponent check, candidate selection, calculation, and a final blunder scan. The sequence should be short enough to use during real games. Its purpose is to prevent predictable errors, not to make every move slow.|Before playing a planned rook lift, notice whether the opponent's last pawn move attacked your bishop. After calculating the lift, visualize its final square and check whether an enemy knight can capture it.|What should your final scan examine?|The exact position after your move: checks against your king, captures of valuable pieces, and forcing threats. Looking only at the current board misses dangers created by your move.|Before releasing a move, let the opponent answer it in your head.
''')

add('02-chess-math',r'''
Chess math: count the whole exchange|Use material arithmetic to compare complete sequences. Assign approximate values P=1, N=3, B=3, R=5, Q=9, then subtract everything you give up from everything you gain. Stop only when immediate recaptures have been considered.|You give a rook and a pawn for a bishop and a knight. The loss is 5+1=6; the gain is 3+3=6. Material is roughly equal, but two active minor pieces may coordinate differently from a rook in an open position.|What is the nominal balance if you win a rook and lose a bishop?|5-3=2 pawn units in your favor. That is winning the exchange. Check for extra pawn losses and king danger before treating the two-point gain as decisive.|Arithmetic evaluates a sequence, not an isolated capture.
Captures can hide a negative sum|Winning the first piece does not mean winning the trade. A common calculation mistake is to count an enemy rook you capture and forget that your queen must then be recaptured. Write a gain-and-loss ledger mentally.|Your queen takes a rook worth 5, and a pawn then takes your queen worth 9. If there is no further gain, the net result is 5-9=-4. The phrase 'I won a rook' describes only half the transaction.|What if a bishop worth 3 captures that rook and is recaptured?|The balance is 5-3=+2. Both sequences capture the same rook, yet their costs differ greatly. That is why the order and identity of attackers matter.|Record the cost of the capturing piece.
Count attackers and legal defenders|A numerical majority of attackers can suggest a profitable exchange, but move order and piece values control the result. A pinned defender may not be able to recapture, while a king cannot capture onto an attacked square.|Three attackers against two defenders is not automatic victory if the first attacker is a queen and the defenders are pawns. Begin with the cheapest legal capture when comparing an ordinary exchange, then test forcing exceptions.|Why can two nominal defenders provide only one legal recapture?|One may be absolutely pinned to its king, or its capture may leave the king checked along another line. Count legal actions in the resulting position.|Count mobility and value alongside the number of defenders.
Two minor pieces versus a rook|A bishop and knight total about six units, compared with a rook's five. Their coordination often makes them strong in a crowded middlegame. A rook may become more effective when open files and widely separated pawns appear.|If you exchange a rook and two pawns for two minor pieces, you give 7 units for 6. That one-unit deficit may be offset by activity, but it is a real cost that must be justified.|What changes when the board contains pawns on both wings and few blockers?|The rook can switch wings quickly and attack distant pawns. Minor pieces may struggle to cover both sides. Material formulas need an assessment of routes and targets.|Material composition matters as much as the total.
Board color is arithmetic|Number files a=1 through h=8. A square is dark when file number plus rank is even, and light when the sum is odd. This gives a quick check for bishop routes and knight color changes.|For c6, 3+6=9, so it is light. For f4, 6+4=10, so it is dark. A bishop on c6 can never reach f4, even if the board is empty, because the colors differ.|What color is h8, and can a bishop from c3 reach it on an empty board?|h8 is dark because 8+8=16. c3 is also dark, and h8 lies on its diagonal: both coordinates increase by five. Matching color is necessary but direct diagonal alignment must also be checked.|Use parity to catch impossible bishop calculations.
Diagonal distance has a test|Two squares lie on the same diagonal when the absolute file difference equals the absolute rank difference. A bishop can travel between them in one move if no piece blocks the path and the destination is legal.|From b2 to g7, the file difference and rank difference are both five. The route passes through c3, d4, e5, and f6. A single blocker on any of those squares stops the bishop.|Are b2 and g6 on one diagonal?|No. Their coordinate differences are five and four. Visual proximity does not make a bishop attack real. Trace each intervening square when a long diagonal matters.|Equal coordinate differences reveal a diagonal.
King distance uses the larger difference|On an empty board, a king needs at least the larger of the file distance and rank distance to travel between squares. Diagonal steps change both coordinates at once. Enemy attacks can increase that minimum or make a route impossible.|From a1 to d4, the king needs three steps: b2, c3, d4. From a1 to e3, it needs four because the file difference is four. The exact path may vary, but no legal path can beat the geometric minimum.|What is the empty-board distance from b2 to f5?|Four king moves: the file difference is four and the rank difference is three. Use this lower bound when judging whether a king can join a pawn race.|Calculate the geometric minimum, then add obstacles.
The knight changes color every time|Every knight move changes square color. An even number of knight moves returns to the original color; an odd number reaches the opposite color. This does not give the full shortest path, but it rules out impossible lengths.|A knight on b1 is on a light square. It cannot reach another light square in three moves. If your imagined three-move route ends on d5, check the route: d5 is light too.|Can a knight move from a1 to b2 in two moves?|No. Although both squares are dark and an even move count is required, no square is a knight move from both a1 and b2. Parity is a necessary test, not a complete route finder.|Use color as a filter, then calculate the actual route.
Centralization increases knight options|A knight on an empty central square may have eight destinations; a corner knight has two. This is a geometric reason for developing toward the center. Occupancy, pawn attacks, and the role of a specific outpost still matter more than raw mobility.|From a1 a knight reaches b3 and c2. From d4 it reaches eight squares. Moving toward the center can multiply options, but a central square controlled by an enemy pawn may be unusable.|Does eight geometric moves mean eight good moves?|No. Some destinations may be occupied by friendly pieces, attacked, tactically losing, or irrelevant. Count legal and useful mobility after checking the empty-board pattern.|Mobility is potential; safe activity turns it into value.
The square of a pawn is a race shortcut|For a freely advancing pawn, compare its remaining moves to promotion with the defending king's distance to interception squares. The familiar square rule visualizes this race. Side to move, the initial double push, blockers, and king support can alter the answer.|A white pawn on a5 needs three pushes to reach a8. A black king on d5 can enter the a5-d8 square and catch an unsupported pawn if its route is clear. A king farther away may fail by one tempo.|Why must you ask whose move it is before using the square rule?|Because the pawn may advance before the king enters the catching area. One tempo can turn an equal-distance race into a promotion. Verify the actual move order near the boundary.|A race calculation starts with the move count and the turn.
Count pawn races in plies|A ply is one player's move. A full move usually contains two plies. Comparing promotion races only by the number of pushes can be misleading because the first promotion may arrive with check and interrupt the other side.|Each side has a pawn one step from promotion. If White moves first and queens with check, Black may have to answer the check instead of promoting. Equal push counts do not imply simultaneous queens.|What three details should you add to a pawn-race count?|Who moves first, whether either promotion gives check, and whether the new piece can stop the rival pawn. Also inspect captures and blocked promotion squares.|Count forcing effects as well as running distance.
Branching explains why calculation needs focus|If you examine three candidates and three replies to each, you already have nine two-ply branches. Adding three continuations per reply produces 27 branches. Practical calculation controls this growth by prioritizing forcing moves and critical defenses.|A line with a forced king reply is easier to visualize than one where the defender has ten quiet choices. This does not make the forcing line best, but it gives you a sensible place to begin your search.|How many leaf positions arise from three choices at each of four plies?|3 x 3 x 3 x 3 = 81. Real chess branching is uneven, so do not calculate every branch equally. Eliminate unsound candidates early and spend effort on plausible resistance.|Use candidate selection to spend calculation where it matters.
Exchange order changes the total|When several pieces attack a square, changing the capture order changes which piece is exposed to recapture. A low-value attacker is often a useful starting point, but checks, pins, and intermediate moves can reverse the usual order.|A pawn and queen both attack an enemy rook. If either can capture safely, a pawn capture risks less material. If the queen capture gives forced mate, king safety overrides that ordinary cost comparison.|Why is 'always capture with the cheapest piece' too strong?|A cheaper capture may release a pin, block your own line, or miss a forcing check. Compare the resulting positions after each legal sequence rather than applying a fixed rule.|Use value order as a hypothesis to calculate.
Tempo has a measurable cost|A tempo is one move's worth of time. You can estimate wasted development by counting repeated moves that do not create compensation. The count is useful only when the opponent can exploit the extra time.|If White moves a knight out, back, and out again while Black develops three different pieces, White has spent two extra turns reaching the same knight placement. Black may use that lead to open the center before White castles.|When might repeating a piece move be justified?|When it wins material, answers a threat, improves a necessary square, or creates a stronger threat than development would. Lost time is a cost, not an automatic verdict.|Compare the benefit of a repeated move with what one tempo buys the opponent.
Pawn islands count connected files|A pawn island is a group of friendly pawns on adjacent files, separated from another group by at least one empty file. Fewer islands often make defense easier. Doubled pawns still occupy a single file and do not create an extra island by themselves.|Pawns on a2, b3, d4, f2, and g3 form three islands: ab, d, and fg. If the d-pawn disappears, two islands remain, but losing a pawn is hardly an improvement by itself.|How many islands are formed by pawns on a2, a3, c2, d3, and h2?|Three: the a-file, the c-d files, and the h-file. Two pawns on a count as one occupied-file group. Then assess whether the gaps can actually be attacked.|Structure statistics need targets and activity to become an evaluation.
A majority is local arithmetic|A pawn majority means more pawns on one region of the board. Three pawns against two may be able to create a passed pawn through exchanges. Blockades, doubled pawns, and king activity determine whether that numerical potential can be realized.|A queenside three-versus-two majority is a useful long-term asset in an endgame. Pushing all three pawns without support can instead leave isolated targets and squares for the opposing king.|Why does a three-versus-two majority not guarantee a passed pawn?|The pawns may be blocked, poorly arranged, or vulnerable to capture. Their advance must produce a favorable exchange sequence, and enemy pieces may stop the resulting passer.|Count the majority, then design the exchanges that make it work.
Space counts useful squares|Space is not simply the number of advanced pawns. Useful space gives your pieces safe squares and makes the opponent's coordination difficult. Counting controlled central squares can help, but a square attacked by a pawn is not automatically a secure outpost.|Pawns on d4 and e4 influence c5, d5, e5, and f5. If Black can attack that center with ...c5 or ...f5, White must be ready to defend or transform it. More space creates more territory to maintain.|What should follow a count of your central pawn controls?|Check which central squares your pieces can actually occupy, which pawn breaks challenge them, and whether your advanced pawns can be defended efficiently.|Measure space by the freedom it creates for pieces.
Training percentages need denominators|A score such as 80 percent says little without the number and difficulty of attempts. Record correct solutions, total attempts, time, and whether you found the complete line. Consistent measurement makes study changes easier to judge.|Eight correct out of ten is 80 percent; eighty out of one hundred is also 80 percent, with more evidence. If one set consists of mate-in-one exercises and the other of defensive combinations, the percentages are not directly comparable.|You solve 18 of 24 puzzles fully. What is your score?|18 divided by 24 equals 0.75, or 75 percent. Separate complete solutions from guessed first moves so the statistic reflects calculation rather than recognition alone.|Compare like with like and keep the sample size.
Allocate study time with a purpose|Time budgets are arithmetic expressions of priorities. A useful schedule assigns minutes to the skill causing your losses and still includes slow play and review. No universal ratio fits every player, so adjust after examining recent games.|For a 60-minute session, 20 minutes of calculation, 20 of game review, 10 of endgames, and 10 of opening repair add to 60. The opening block should answer a concrete recurring problem rather than grow indefinitely.|If review expands to 30 minutes, how can the session remain one hour?|For example use 15 calculation, 30 review, 10 endgame, and 5 opening minutes. The change deliberately favors diagnosis. Reassess after several games to see whether the identified errors decline.|Make study minutes serve observed weaknesses.
Separate evaluation from certainty|An engine score estimates a position under its search and model; it is not a proof of your practical winning chances. A numerical edge can depend on difficult moves. Mate announcements and exact tablebase results have different meanings from an ordinary score.|A position evaluated at +1 may require several precise defenses to retain the advantage. A simpler position with a smaller score may be easier for you to handle, but simplicity does not change objective tactics.|Why should you not interpret +2 as 'two guaranteed extra pawns'?|The score summarizes many factors and is expressed on a pawn-like scale. It may reflect attack, activity, or long-term pressure instead of an actual material surplus.|Use numerical evaluations to ask questions, then inspect the line.
''')

add('03-opening-decisions',r'''
When to learn chess openings|Learn opening principles as soon as you can play legal games. Add a small repertoire once you regularly reach playable positions and can explain basic development. Deep memorization becomes useful when recurring positions in your own games demand precise answers; it need not wait for a particular rating.|After repeatedly meeting 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6, choose one sensible setup and study its central pawn plan. If you lose mainly to hanging pieces on move twelve, tactical checking will usually help more than memorizing another twenty moves.|What should trigger your next opening study session?|A recurring practical question from a game: where a piece belongs, which pawn break works, or why an early tactic failed. Learn the answer and a short supporting line, then test it in play.|Start with principles; deepen theory in response to real positions.
Build a small usable repertoire|A repertoire is a set of positions you understand and can reach against common replies. Begin with a manageable first move as White and a response to both 1.e4 and 1.d4 as Black. Learn plans, tactical warnings, and typical structures alongside moves.|A player may choose 1.e4, then prepare an answer to ...e5, the Sicilian, the French, and the Caro-Kann. Each branch needs a workable starting plan, not an encyclopedia of sidelines.|What belongs on a one-page repertoire note?|A short legal move sequence, intended piece squares, a central pawn break, one tactical danger, and the position where memorization ends. Explain why each move is played.|Prefer a small repertoire you can explain to a large one you can only recite.
Develop with a job in mind|Development means bringing pieces into useful play. A piece is not well developed merely because it left its starting square. Its destination should influence the center, help king safety, support a break, or restrain the opponent.|After 1.e4 e5, 2.Nf3 attacks e5 and helps White castle. It develops with a purpose. A move such as 2.Nh3 also moves a knight, but usually contributes less to central play and may need another move later.|What question should accompany every developing move?|What useful task does this piece perform on its new square, and can the opponent chase it while gaining time? Several developing moves may be sound; compare their jobs.|Develop toward tasks, not just away from the back rank.
Occupy and challenge the center|Central squares give pieces routes toward both wings. You can influence them with pawns or pieces, but you must notice when the opponent can undermine your support. A large pawn center needs development behind it.|After 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.d4, White immediately challenges Black's central pawn. If Black captures on d4, White must recapture or otherwise justify the pawn loss; opening the center creates concrete obligations.|Why is pushing two central pawns not a complete opening strategy?|Those pawns can become targets. Pieces must develop, the king needs safety, and central exchanges must be calculated. Occupation without support can leave a fragile center.|Build a center that your pieces can maintain or transform.
Castle for the position you have|Castling usually improves king safety and rook coordination, but its value depends on the pawn shelter and enemy attack. Decide which side is safer rather than treating a move number as a deadline. Delaying without a concrete reason can become costly.|If your kingside pawns have advanced far and the opponent has open files there, kingside castling may enter the attack. Queenside castling also needs an inspection of the c-file, diagonal access, and the king's future escape squares.|What should you inspect before choosing a castling side?|The pawn cover, open lines, nearby enemy pieces, and how quickly your opponent can open more lines. Compare that with the danger of leaving the king in the center.|Castle into safety, not merely out of habit.
Avoid unnecessary repeated moves|Repeated moves by one piece consume time that might develop the rest of your army. This matters most when the center can open. Repeat a move when a concrete threat or gain justifies it, not because you have run out of ideas.|A bishop chased twice by pawn moves may cost tempi, but those pawn moves also leave weaknesses. Decide whether keeping the bishop's pressure is worth the time and whether you can exploit the new pawn structure.|When is retreating a developed piece the right choice?|When it is attacked, badly placed, or needed for a sound plan. The goal is useful coordination, so preserving an active piece can outweigh a general development guideline.|Account for lost time without becoming a prisoner of slogans.
Opening pawn grabs need an exit plan|Taking a pawn can cost development or trap the capturing piece. Before grabbing, calculate the opponent's forcing replies and identify a safe route home. Material gains are strongest when they do not hand over an enduring initiative.|A queen that takes b2 may win a pawn but face rook moves, bishop attacks, and blocked retreat squares. The capture is sometimes theoretical and sound; the point is to calculate the cage, not label every b-pawn poisoned.|What makes an opening pawn 'poisoned'?|Accepting it allows compensation stronger than the material gain, such as a trapped queen, lost development, or an attack on the king. Demonstrate the concrete continuation before using the label.|Win pawns you can keep without losing the initiative for free.
Meet an unfamiliar move with questions|An unusual opening move need not be refuted immediately. Identify what it attacks, what it neglects, and what squares it weakens. A sound developing move may be the best answer if no tactic exists.|After an early flank pawn move, check whether your central pawn advance gains space safely. Do not launch a speculative sacrifice merely because the opponent left your memorized line. Their move may be playable but slightly slow.|What should replace memory when the book line ends?|Use king safety, development, central control, and calculation of immediate threats. These criteria remain available in every opening, including positions you have never seen.|Treat surprise as a position to assess, not a demand to punish.
Learn the reason behind move order|Two setups that look similar may differ because one move permits a tactic before the other piece is developed. Move order determines which options each side retains. Study the opponent's best response between your intended moves.|In an open game, castling before grabbing a central pawn may remove a pin against your king. Capturing first can leave the same piece tactically vulnerable. The final setup is irrelevant if the intermediate position loses material.|How can you test whether two opening moves commute?|Play them in both orders and examine the opponent's checks, captures, and central breaks after the first move. If the available reply changes, the order has strategic or tactical meaning.|Evaluate the position between your planned moves.
Central breaks need support|A pawn break challenges the opponent's structure and can free cramped pieces. Before playing it, prepare the necessary recaptures and identify the lines that will open. Premature breaks may leave isolated pawns or expose your king.|The move d4 in an open king-pawn game often releases the c1 bishop and contests e5. But if a pin makes the intended recapture impossible, d4 may simply lose a pawn. Development and tactics decide the timing.|What three things belong in a pawn-break check?|The capture sequence, the resulting pawn structure, and which side benefits from the opened files or diagonals. Include king safety before declaring the break thematic.|A thematic break is an idea to verify, not an automatic move.
Understand the open-game starting point|After 1.e4 e5, both sides have space and access to developing squares. The struggle often concerns the central tension and rapid piece activity. Learn typical pressure on e4, e5, f2, and f7 before studying long variations.|The sequence 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 develops knights and contests e5. White can then choose bishop development or a central challenge. Each choice shapes which pieces can enter quickly and how the center may open.|What is Black doing with 2...Nc6 besides developing?|Defending the e5 pawn and influencing d4. The knight also occupies c6, which affects future use of the c-pawn. Even natural moves have both benefits and constraints.|Read opening moves as bundles of concrete functions.
The Italian bishop has a target|After 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4, the bishop develops actively and aims toward f7. That pressure supports tactics but does not create a forced win by itself. White still needs a safe king and a central plan.|A quiet setup with d3 supports e4 and develops the c1 bishop gradually. A later d4 break may challenge Black's center. If you aim for a quick attack on f7, count defenders and calculate checks rather than assuming the target is weak enough.|Why is f7 called sensitive in the starting position?|Initially only Black's king defends it. As pieces develop, that can change, and sacrifices there may be unsound. Recount the actual defenders in the actual position.|A familiar target is an invitation to calculate.
The Spanish bishop applies indirect pressure|After 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bb5, White pressures the knight that helps defend e5. The bishop's role is often long-term rather than an immediate pawn win. Black has tactical and structural ways to meet that pressure.|Following 3...a6 4.Bxc6 dxc6, a greedy 5.Nxe5 can be met by 5...Qd4, attacking the knight and e4. The apparent removal of a defender does not automatically win a clean pawn.|What lesson does the ...Qd4 reply teach?|After removing a defender, calculate the opponent's active reply instead of assuming the target is free. Opening tactics often compensate for a temporary weakness.|Pressure can be useful even when the immediate capture fails.
The Sicilian creates an imbalance|After 1.e4 c5, Black challenges d4 from the flank rather than mirroring White's e-pawn. In many open Sicilian structures, Black's c-pawn trades for White's d-pawn. That changes central pawn numbers and opens the c-file.|After 1.e4 c5 2.Nf3 d6 3.d4 cxd4 4.Nxd4, White develops quickly while Black has two central pawns against White's e-pawn. These features explain competing plans but do not decide the position alone.|What file becomes half-open for Black after the sample exchange?|The c-file: Black's c-pawn has left it while White usually retains a c-pawn. Rook pressure there can become useful after development. White may seek initiative before Black coordinates.|Connect an opening's pawn trade to its later plans.
The French asks about pawn chains|After 1.e4 e6 2.d4 d5, Black challenges White's center and develops a solid pawn structure. In the Advance line 3.e5, the center closes and pawn-chain attacks become central to planning. The blocked c8 bishop needs a future role.|After 3...c5, Black attacks the d4 base supporting e5. White may support the chain with c3 while completing development. Both sides should prepare their breaks rather than shuffle pieces behind the pawns indefinitely.|Why attack d4 instead of trying only to capture e5?|Weakening the chain's base can undermine the advanced pawn indirectly and open useful lines. Direct pressure on the head may also work, but the base is an important structural target.|Study the pawn chain to understand the opening plan.
The Caro-Kann gives a bishop a route|After 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5, Black challenges the center while keeping the c8 bishop's diagonal open for now. That bishop's development is one reason the move ...e6 is often delayed. The c-pawn's location also influences later central breaks.|In 3.e5 Bf5, Black develops the bishop outside the pawn chain before playing ...e6. The bishop may become a target for White's pawns, so Black must combine development with a plan for its retreat.|What is the tradeoff in playing ...c6 before ...d5?|Black supports the d5 pawn, but c6 is unavailable to the b8 knight and another tempo may be needed for ...c5 later. The structure offers advantages at a cost.|Opening choices exchange one set of conveniences for another.
The Queen's Gambit challenges support|After 1.d4 d5 2.c4, White attacks the d5 pawn's support structure and offers a flank pawn. The goal is central influence and activity, not a promise to sacrifice a pawn permanently in every variation.|If Black captures with 2...dxc4, the d-pawn leaves the center. White can develop and often recover c4 later. Trying to keep that pawn at any cost can leave Black behind in development or create queenside targets.|What should White evaluate before rushing to recover c4?|Development, king safety, and whether Black can safely hold the extra pawn. A useful developing move may be more valuable than an immediate queen move just to restore material equality.|Central compensation can justify a temporary pawn investment.
Fianchettoes need diagonal awareness|A bishop developed to g2, b2, g7, or b7 can influence a long diagonal. Its power depends on blockers and central pawn breaks. Moving the nearby pawn also changes the king's shelter and creates squares that may need defense.|A bishop on g2 behind a white pawn on e4 may be temporarily restricted along g2-f3-e4. Advancing or exchanging the central pawn can release it. Trading the fianchettoed bishop may leave weak squares near a castled king.|What should you inspect before exchanging your fianchettoed bishop?|Whether other pieces can defend its color complex and whether the opponent can use the long diagonal or nearby holes. The exchange may still be good if it removes a stronger attacker.|Judge the bishop together with its diagonal and king shelter.
Traps are lessons in causes|An opening trap is useful when it teaches a recurring tactical cause: a loose piece, an unsafe king, or a pinned defender. Memorizing a trick without understanding its conditions makes you vulnerable when the opponent changes one move.|The sequence 1.f3 e5 2.g4 Qh4# demonstrates how moving the f- and g-pawns can expose the king's diagonal. Its value is the king-safety lesson, not the expectation that opponents will repeat the same moves.|What should you extract from studying a trap?|The vulnerable square, the missing defender, and the move that opened the line. Then ask which defensive move would remove the combination before it begins.|Learn why a trap works and when it stops working.
Repair your repertoire from your games|After a game, locate the first position you did not understand, rather than merely the first move outside theory. Analyze it yourself, compare reliable analysis, and record one practical improvement. Replaying the repaired position helps turn the note into a habit.|If you reach the same structure three times and each time misplace your queen, study her useful squares and the pawn break they support. Adding another opening variation would not directly solve that recurring problem.|What makes a useful opening repair note?|It states the problem, explains a better plan, gives a short legal example, and names the tactical condition that makes the plan work. Keep it short enough to review before playing.|Let your games choose the next opening question.
''')

add('04-positional-judgment',r'''
Improve your worst piece|When no immediate tactic demands action, identify the piece contributing least to the position. Look for a route to a useful square and check whether you have time to make it. This turns vague maneuvering into a specific improvement.|A bishop trapped behind its own central pawns may need a pawn break rather than another bishop move. A knight on the rim may need two moves to reach a stable central square. Diagnose the cause of inactivity first.|How do you choose between two possible improving moves?|Compare the task each piece gains, the time required, and the opponent's best response. Favor improvements that also defend a weakness or prepare a useful pawn break.|Give an inactive piece a job and a safe route to it.
An outpost must resist pawns|An outpost is a useful square that enemy pawns cannot easily challenge, often supported by your own pawn. A knight can become powerful there, but the value depends on nearby targets and whether the opponent can exchange it.|A white knight on d5 may be stable if Black lacks c- and e-pawns able to attack it. If Black can play ...c6 safely, d5 may be a temporary station rather than a permanent outpost.|What should you check beyond whether a square is pawn-safe?|Can the piece be traded favorably, does it attack meaningful targets, and can it retreat if the structure changes? A secure square with no useful influence may be overrated.|An outpost combines stability with purpose.
Trade a bad piece for an active one|An exchange can improve your position by changing relative piece quality even when material stays equal. Compare what each piece currently does and what it could do after the trade. Labels such as 'good bishop' need concrete targets.|If your restricted bishop can exchange for the opponent's active knight, your remaining pieces may gain freedom. But if that bishop is the only defender of key dark squares around your king, the exchange can be dangerous.|What is the essential before-and-after comparison?|Identify which tasks disappear on each side and which weaknesses remain. The best trade removes an important enemy function without creating a more serious problem for you.|Exchange functions, not just equal point values.
Open files need a destination|A rook on an open file can pressure the opponent's position, but file control alone does not win. Find a penetration square, prepare support, and calculate exchanges. Sometimes a rook belongs behind a passed pawn instead.|If your rook enters the seventh rank, it may attack pawns from the side and restrict the enemy king. If entry simply allows a rook trade into a drawn ending, maintaining tension may be stronger.|What should you name before committing both rooks to one file?|The intended entry square and the target after entry. Then inspect how the opponent can contest the file or exchange the attacking rooks.|A rook operation needs a destination and a reason to go there.
Half-open files point toward pawns|A half-open file has no friendly pawn but contains an enemy pawn. It can guide rook pressure toward a fixed target. The target's defenders and the possibility of a pawn advance determine whether pressure can be increased.|After your c-pawn trades away, a rook on c1 may attack an enemy pawn on c7. If that pawn can advance safely to c5, it may escape the pressure and challenge your center instead.|How can you strengthen pressure against a half-open-file target?|Prevent its freeing advance, add another attacker, or force a defender away through a second threat. Do not pile up attackers without checking tactical counterplay.|A target is most useful when it cannot move out of danger.
Weak pawns need attackers|An isolated or backward pawn is a potential weakness, not an automatic losing defect. To exploit it, you need access, sufficient attackers, and time. The owner may gain open lines or active pieces in return.|An isolated d-pawn can support active pieces on e5 and c5 while creating attacking chances. If pieces are exchanged and the pawn becomes fixed, its defensive burden may grow. The same pawn has different value in different phases.|Why might the side with an isolated pawn avoid exchanges?|Active pieces can use the space and open lines around it. Trading them may leave a static weakness with little compensation. Calculate specific exchanges instead of following a blanket rule.|A structural weakness matters when the opponent can organize against it.
Blockade before attacking a passer|A passed pawn cannot be stopped by an enemy pawn on its file or adjacent files ahead of it. Blocking it can prevent promotion and limit the support it receives. A knight often makes a useful blockader because it can attack while sitting directly in front.|A knight on d6 in front of a white pawn on d5 stops its advance while influencing nearby squares. The blockade becomes fragile if White can chase or exchange that knight, so support the blockading square.|What should the side with the passed pawn try to do?|Challenge the blockader, create a second threat, or use the pawn's restriction to improve other pieces. Pushing the pawn is only one way to benefit from it.|First immobilize the passer; then organize pressure on it.
Attack the base of a pawn chain|A pawn chain is a diagonal group of pawns supporting one another. The base has no pawn behind it in the chain, making it a natural pressure point. Attacking the head can also work when opening lines is more urgent.|White pawns on c3, d4, and e5 form a chain based at c3. Black might attack d4 with ...c5 to undermine e5, or prepare pressure on c3 if the position allows. The best target depends on access.|Why can undermining a supporter be stronger than attacking the advanced pawn directly?|The advanced pawn may be well protected, while its support is easier to challenge. Removing support can force several weaknesses to appear together.|Trace pawn support backward to find useful break points.
Choose the right pawn break|A pawn break is often the main strategic lever in a closed position. Choose one that opens lines for your better-placed pieces or attacks an overextended chain. Prepare it so the resulting captures do not create a worse weakness.|If your rooks are aimed at the kingside and your king is secure, an f-pawn break may open useful files. If your pieces are aimed elsewhere, the same push can merely weaken your shelter and leave a pawn exposed.|What determines the timing of a break?|Piece readiness, the opponent's counterplay, and the forced capture sequence. A break becomes stronger when your pieces already occupy the lines it is about to open.|Prepare pieces for the position after the pawns move.
Two weaknesses stretch a defense|A single weakness can sometimes be defended efficiently. Creating a second target may force the defender to split resources. The targets must be far enough apart or tactically connected so that one defensive setup cannot handle both.|A rook tied to defending a queenside pawn may struggle when your king attacks a kingside pawn. Switching pressure can force a concession, but switching too slowly may let the opponent activate the rook with counterplay.|What makes a second weakness useful rather than cosmetic?|It demands a different defensive resource or creates a forcing threat. A pawn that cannot be attacked is not an effective second target, even if its structure looks imperfect.|Create demands the opponent cannot satisfy with the same pieces.
Prophylaxis starts with the opponent|Prophylaxis means recognizing and limiting the opponent's useful plan. It is active thinking, not passive waiting. Prevent a move when the restriction is valuable enough to justify the time and does not concede a stronger plan elsewhere.|If the opponent wants to occupy an open file, contesting its entry square may be more efficient than immediately doubling your rooks. If a knight wants an outpost, a timely pawn move may deny it before arrival.|How do you avoid making unnecessary preventive moves?|Name the opponent's intended move and its consequence. Compare prevention with creating a stronger threat of your own. Prevent real plans, not every imaginable possibility.|A good preventive move answers a specific future problem.
Space asks for controlled expansion|More space can improve mobility and restrict enemy pieces, but advanced pawns need defense. Keep enough tension to stop the opponent from exchanging cramped pieces freely, while remaining alert to freeing breaks.|If you hold a broad center, exchanging all central pawns may release the opponent's bishops and rooks. On the other hand, refusing an exchange that loses a pawn is not a sensible way to preserve space.|What should a cramped side often seek?|Useful exchanges and freeing pawn breaks. The side with more space should recognize those plans and decide which can be prevented without overextending.|Use space to improve coordination while watching the opponent's release valves.
Do not mistake activity for appearance|A piece is active when it performs useful work: attacking targets, controlling entry squares, supporting threats, or restricting enemy pieces. A central location can look impressive while accomplishing little. Judge influence against the position's needs.|A knight on e5 may dominate if it attacks weaknesses and cannot be chased. If it only looks central while a rook invades your second rank, defending the invasion may matter more than preserving the knight's pose.|How can you test whether a piece is really active?|Name the concrete moves or restrictions it creates. If removing it changes little, its apparent activity may be less important than another piece's defensive role.|Measure activity through tasks and consequences.
Good bishops and bad bishops are relative|A bishop restricted by its own pawns on the same color is often called bad. That bishop can still defend important pawns or become strong after a break. A bishop with open diagonals can be ineffective if no targets lie on its color.|A dark-squared bishop behind dark-square pawns may be passive in the middlegame. If it protects every vulnerable pawn and cannot be exchanged safely, it may be an essential defender rather than a piece to sacrifice casually.|What can improve a restricted bishop without moving it?|Advance or exchange a blocking pawn, open a diagonal, or create targets on its color. Evaluate the pawn change before assuming the bishop must be traded.|A bishop's prospects depend on the structure you can create.
Knights like stable targets|Knights thrive near fixed weaknesses because their short range is less of a problem there. They can jump pawn chains and occupy blockading squares. Their effectiveness drops when targets are far apart and the route between them is slow.|A knight near a fixed isolated pawn can attack and blockade it. In an endgame with passed pawns on opposite wings, the same knight may spend too many moves switching sides while a bishop crosses the board quickly.|What should you ask before trading a bishop for a knight?|Will the remaining structure be closed or open, are targets fixed or mobile, and which piece can reach them efficiently? Also check tactical reasons that override the structural preference.|Choose the minor piece that fits the future pawn map.
Color complexes survive piece trades|When a bishop is exchanged, the squares it defended do not disappear. Weaknesses on that color may become harder to protect. Before trading a bishop near your king, inspect pawn holes and possible enemy queen or knight entries.|If your dark-square pawns have advanced and your dark-squared bishop is traded, squares such as f3 or h3 may need new defenders. The problem depends on the exact pawn arrangement and enemy access, not on color terminology alone.|How can you compensate for losing a key bishop?|Trade the opponent's matching attacker, use other pieces to control entry squares, or alter the pawn structure safely. Sometimes exchanging queens removes the danger most directly.|After a trade, ask who inherits the abandoned squares.
Keep tension when it preserves choices|Pawn or piece tension gives each side the option to capture. Releasing it can clarify the position but also free the opponent. Maintain tension when your position benefits from flexibility and no tactical reason forces a decision.|Two central pawns attack one another. Capturing may open a file for the opponent's rook; advancing may close your own bishop; waiting may preserve both options. Calculate each transformation before choosing.|When should you resolve tension immediately?|When a favorable tactic exists, the opponent threatens to exploit it, or the resulting structure clearly serves your plan. 'Keep tension' is not a reason to ignore a forcing move.|Value the options tension preserves, then choose the best transformation.
Exchange when the ending suits you|Trading queens or rooks can reduce danger and make an advantage easier to convert. It can also remove the pieces that attack an enemy weakness. Evaluate the resulting ending before offering an exchange.|With an extra pawn and an active king, a queen trade may simplify winning chances. If your extra pawn is a rook pawn with the wrong bishop, simplifying into that ending may throw the advantage away.|What should you inspect before accepting a simplifying trade?|King activity, pawn structure, passed pawns, remaining piece quality, and known drawing resources. Count material after the trade, but do not stop there.|Simplify into a favorable position, not merely a smaller one.
An initiative must become something|The initiative means your threats make the opponent respond. It often rests on temporary activity or development. Use it to gain material, improve the structure, achieve a lasting restriction, or build a decisive attack before the opponent coordinates.|A lead in development may justify opening the center while the enemy king remains there. If you spend several tempi on unrelated pawn moves, the opponent may castle and erase the reason your attack was promising.|What should you ask while you have the initiative?|What concrete asset can this pressure produce, and how much time remains before the opponent neutralizes it? Calculate the transition rather than attacking for its own sake.|Convert temporary time into a durable gain.
Make a plan that can change|A useful plan identifies a target, a method, and a reason the position supports it. It should be revised when the opponent changes the structure or creates a forcing threat. Consistency is valuable only while the underlying conditions remain.|You plan a queenside minority attack to create a weak pawn. The opponent opens the center against your uncastled king. Continuing the pawn march without addressing the center ignores the changed position.|What should cause you to revise a plan?|A new forcing threat, a changed pawn structure, an exchange of a key piece, or loss of access to the target. Re-evaluate the reason for the plan rather than mechanically following its next move.|Keep the objective flexible and the calculation concrete.
''')

add('05-endgame-technique',r'''
Activate the king safely|In many endgames the king becomes a strong attacking and defending piece because fewer enemy pieces can generate a mating attack. Bring it toward the center or a critical pawn while checking every approach square for tactics.|A king on g1 may need several moves to reach d4. Starting that journey before the opponent creates a passed pawn can decide whether you can stop it. Remaining behind your pawns without a reason may waste your strongest available defender.|What makes a king route safe enough to begin?|Identify enemy checks, protected entry squares, and whether your departure abandons a pawn or mating threat. The absence of queens helps, but rooks and minor pieces can still punish an exposed king.|Use the king actively after checking the opponent's forcing play.
Opposition is control of entry|Kings facing one another with one square between them create direct opposition. The side not to move often benefits because the other king must yield ground. The value depends on pawns, board edges, and the squares each side wants to enter.|White king e4 and Black king e6 have e5 between them. If Black must move, White may gain access to d5 or f5, depending on Black's choice and the rest of the board. If White must move, the roles reverse.|Why is opposition a tool rather than the goal itself?|You want access to critical squares or to prevent the opponent's access. Winning a geometric opposition that leads nowhere useful may waste time or allow a pawn break.|Use opposition to win a square that matters.
Distant opposition is about timing|Kings can oppose each other from farther away. On the same file or rank, an odd number of intervening squares can preserve the opposition relationship as they approach, provided the route and pawn moves permit it. Always connect the geometry to entry squares.|White king e2 and Black king e6 have three squares between them. If Black must move and no pawn moves change the tempo, White can try to preserve the relationship while approaching. Other routes or obstacles may change the result.|What can break a simple opposition calculation?|A reserve pawn move, a diagonal approach, a blocked square, or a tactical pawn capture. Calculate actual legal routes rather than relying only on the count of intervening squares.|Track available tempi as well as king alignment.
Key squares matter more than chasing|In a king-and-pawn ending, key squares are squares where the stronger king can often secure promotion with correct play. Their identity depends on the pawn's file and rank. Rook pawns have important exceptions because the board edge limits access.|With a white pawn on e5, squares on the sixth rank in front of it can be critical for the white king. Reaching such squares can be more useful than directly chasing the defending king sideways.|Why should you avoid pushing a pawn before checking king access?|The push may change the opposition, remove a waiting move, or let the defender blockade it. Improve the king first when that secures the pawn's advance.|Win access for the king before spending the pawn's tempo.
Reserve pawn moves are resources|A pawn move can act as a waiting move that changes whose turn it is in a king confrontation. Spending every harmless-looking pawn move too early can leave you in zugzwang later. Keep track of which moves preserve the structure.|If your king has the desired opposition but must move, a safe pawn move on the other wing may force the opponent to yield instead. That pawn move must not create a losing weakness or accelerate an enemy passer.|What should you record when evaluating a pawn ending?|Useful waiting moves for both sides, including single versus double pawn advances when legal. A tempo that looks spare may be essential to controlling an entry square.|Treat pawn tempi like a limited reserve.
Triangulation changes the turn|Triangulation is a maneuver that returns to a useful arrangement while changing whose turn it is. It works when one king has a three-square route and the other cannot mirror it without conceding something. Not every king position permits such a maneuver.|A king may move through three nearby squares to return while the opposing king has only two safe waiting squares. The resulting turn change can force the defender away from a critical pawn or entry point.|What must be verified before claiming a triangulation works?|Every square on the route is safe, the opponent's replies are accounted for, and the final turn change produces a concrete gain. A diagram resemblance is not proof.|A waiting maneuver must end with a useful zugzwang.
Rook pawns have edge exceptions|A rook pawn offers fewer routes for the attacking king because one side of the pawn is off the board. A defending king that reaches the promotion corner can often draw a bare king-and-rook-pawn ending. Exact king placement still matters.|A white a-pawn promotes on a8. If Black's king occupies that corner and cannot be forced away, advancing the pawn may produce stalemate rather than victory. White cannot use squares to the left of the a-file to outflank.|Why are general central-pawn rules unreliable for rook pawns?|The board edge removes key approach squares and creates stalemate resources. Calculate the actual corner position instead of transferring a central-pawn rule unchanged.|Check rook-pawn exceptions before simplifying.
The wrong bishop and rook pawn|A bishop may be unable to help a rook pawn promote when it cannot control the promotion corner and the defending king reaches that corner. This is a major drawing resource, but it depends on the exact position and absence of other winning resources.|White's a-pawn promotes on dark a8. A light-squared bishop cannot drive a black king from a8 by controlling that square. If the king is securely established there, extra material may not be enough to win.|What should you inspect before trading into bishop and rook pawn versus king?|The bishop's color, the promotion square's color, the defending king's access to the corner, and whether other pawns can change the situation. Prevent the fortress before it is established.|Match the bishop to the promotion square before simplifying.
Outside passers can distract a king|An outside passed pawn lies far from the main pawn mass. Its value may be the time it forces the enemy king to spend catching it. Your king can then invade the other wing. The race must be calculated precisely.|A queenside passer may draw the defending king away while your king attacks kingside pawns. Sacrificing the passer can be correct if the resulting captures and promotion race favor you, but distance alone does not guarantee success.|What should you calculate before sacrificing an outside passer?|How many moves the defender needs to capture it, how many pawns your king can win in that time, and whether the final pawn race includes checking promotions.|A passer can win by distracting, not only by promoting.
Connected passers support each other|Passed pawns on adjacent files can protect one another as they advance. Their strength rises when the defending king cannot blockade both or when promotion threats gain tempi. Careless advancement can separate them and remove their mutual support.|White pawns on e5 and f5 can create two promotion threats. Pushing one far ahead may let the enemy king blockade the other and capture the advanced pawn. The correct order depends on king position and tactical checks.|What should guide the order of connected-pawn advances?|Maintain support, restrict the defending king, and calculate whether either promotion comes with check. The most advanced pawn is not always the one to push next.|Coordinate passed pawns as a pair.
Rooks belong behind many passers|A rook behind a passed pawn often becomes more active as the pawn advances, whether the pawn is friendly or hostile. This is a useful guideline, not an absolute rule. Side checks, promotion tactics, and king shelter can favor another placement.|Behind your own passer, the rook supports each forward step. Behind an enemy passer, it can attack the pawn while retaining checking distance. A rook in front may be tied down and lose room as the pawn advances.|When can the guideline fail?|When a tactical promotion threat, a safe blockade, or a stronger checking position requires a different square. Calculate immediate moves before relocating a rook just to match the rule.|Use rook placement to preserve activity as the pawn moves.
An active rook can save a weak ending|Passive defense lets the stronger side improve without resistance. An active rook can check the king, attack pawns, and force concessions even while down material. Activity must still respect mating threats and the safety of essential pawns.|A rook tied to defending a pawn from the side may have no counterplay. Sacrificing that pawn to reach the seventh rank can sometimes create enough checks and pawn threats to hold the ending.|How should you evaluate a pawn sacrifice for rook activity?|Compare the resulting checking opportunities and pawn targets with the passive position's long-term prospects. Do not assume activity compensates; identify the concrete defensive resources.|Seek counterplay that gives the opponent decisions to solve.
Cut off the king with a rook|A rook can deny an entire rank or file to the enemy king. This can keep the king away from a passed pawn or reduce the defending side's coordination. Maintain the cutoff only while the rook itself remains safe and useful.|A rook controlling a file between the enemy king and your passer can force the king to take a longer route. If you abandon that file for a minor pawn gain, the king may reach the blockade in time.|What should you calculate before moving a cutting-off rook?|Whether the enemy king can cross the barrier on the next move and whether that entry changes the pawn race or fortress. Sometimes maintaining restriction is worth more than a pawn.|A rook's barrier can be more valuable than its immediate capture.
Checking distance helps rook defense|A defending rook often needs distance from the enemy king to give repeated checks without being attacked. Checks from too close can let the king approach with tempo. The available ranks and files determine which checking method works.|If the attacking king can step toward a nearby rook while escaping check, the rook may run out of safe checking squares. From farther away, the same rook can continue checking along the rank while preserving room.|What should a defender inspect before beginning side checks?|The number of files between rook and king, the king's escape direction, and whether the pawn can shield checks. Exact rook-ending rules depend on the pawn file and piece placement.|Give the rook enough room to keep checking safely.
Build shelter before advancing the king|In rook endings, a king supporting a passed pawn may be exposed to endless checks. A useful winning plan often creates shelter with the rook or pawn before the king steps forward. Familiar bridge-building ideas must be matched to exact positions.|A rook can move to a rank where it will later interpose against checks, allowing the king to advance. If the rook moves too early and loses the pawn or permits a checking skewer, the plan fails.|What should you verify in a bridge-building plan?|The defender's checking routes, the pawn's safety, and a legal interposition square after the king advances. Calculate the checks instead of assuming a memorized setup applies.|Prepare an answer to checks before moving into them.
Opposite-colored bishops change priorities|Bishops on opposite colors cannot directly challenge one another's squares. In pure bishop endings this can support blockades and drawing chances. With queens or rooks present, the same color imbalance can strengthen an attack on undefended squares.|A defending bishop may hold a diagonal that blocks passed pawns while the enemy bishop cannot exchange it. If heavy pieces remain, the attacking queen can join the bishop against a king whose matching-color defender is absent.|Why is 'opposite bishops mean draw' misleading?|Pawn placement, king activity, passed pawns, and remaining pieces can make the position winning or losing. Identify a concrete blockade or defensive method before claiming a draw.|Evaluate the whole position, not just the bishops' colors.
Knights struggle with distant passers|A knight's short range makes it difficult to control passed pawns far apart. A pawn near the edge also limits some knight blockade routes. However, forks and checking captures can reverse an apparently lost race.|A knight stopping an a-pawn may be too far away to catch an h-pawn. Before resigning, inspect whether a checking fork can win a pawn or whether the enemy king must abandon support to avoid it.|How do you evaluate a knight's defensive route?|Count actual legal knight moves to blockade or capture, track square colors, and include forcing checks. Geometric distance measured in king moves does not describe a knight's travel time.|Calculate knight routes explicitly in pawn races.
Queen endings demand perpetual-check awareness|Queens can generate checks across large distances, so king safety remains central even with few pawns. Advancing a passer without shelter may allow perpetual check. Exchanging queens can be decisive if the pawn ending is favorable.|You may be a pawn ahead with an advanced passer, yet unable to escape repeated checks. A queen move that offers a favorable exchange or creates a safe king square can be stronger than another pawn push.|What should you check before forcing a queen exchange?|The resulting king-and-pawn ending: opposition, pawn races, and rook-pawn exceptions. A winning-looking queen trade can lead straight to a drawn or lost pawn ending.|Calculate both the checking defense and the simplified ending.
Convert with the enemy counterplay in view|A winning advantage still requires accurate moves. First limit the opponent's active resources, then improve pieces and create a clear conversion plan. Avoid unnecessary complications when a safe winning route exists, but do not trade into a known draw.|With an extra rook, eliminate dangerous checks and secure your king before collecting every pawn. With only an extra pawn, activating the king may matter more than forcing exchanges that reduce winning chances.|What is a useful question in a winning ending?|What is the opponent's most dangerous remaining resource: a passer, perpetual checks, stalemate, or a fortress? Choose moves that reduce it while preserving your own winning mechanism.|Win by controlling resistance, not by admiring the extra material.
Know what you are trying to hold|A defender needs a concrete drawing method, not merely stubborn waiting. Possible resources include blockade, checking from a distance, reaching a promotion corner, or creating stalemate. Choose the method that fits the exact material and structure.|In one ending the king must run toward a rook-pawn corner; in another it must stay active to stop a central passer. Following a generic 'keep the king central' rule can miss the only saving route.|How should you study a defensive ending after a loss?|Find the last position with a clear drawing method, identify the move that abandoned it, and practice both sides from that point. Learn the mechanism and its limits.|A draw is often built around one precise defensive resource.
''')

add('06-thinking-and-training',r'''
Generate candidates before committing|A candidate move is a serious option worth calculating. Begin with forcing moves and the opponent's threats, then add a quiet improvement when appropriate. Comparing two or three plausible ideas is often more effective than calculating the first attractive move deeply.|You see a tempting capture, but a checking move may gain a more valuable piece. Listing both before calculation prevents the first idea from monopolizing your attention. The best candidate may still be the quiet defensive move.|When should candidate generation be short?|When you are in check or a forcing threat limits the legal answers. Even then, inspect all reasonable defenses before committing. The goal is useful alternatives, not a fixed number of moves.|Give a good-looking move a competitor.
Checks, captures, and threats form a scan|Forcing moves narrow the opponent's choices, so they are useful starting points for calculation. Scan checks, captures, and direct threats for both sides. They are candidates rather than commands; many checks are harmless or losing.|A check that drives the enemy king to safety may help the defender. A quiet move threatening mate can be stronger if it also stops the opponent's counterplay. Compare consequences after the defender's best answer.|Why scan the opponent's checks before beginning your own attack?|A checking reply can interrupt your intended sequence and win material or force mate. Your attack only works if the opponent cannot use a stronger forcing resource first.|Prioritize forcing moves, then judge their outcomes.
Calculate the opponent's best resistance|A combination is only as strong as its answer to the strongest defense. Do not assume the opponent will capture a sacrificed piece or follow the most natural recapture. Search for checks, counterattacks, and declining the sacrifice.|You offer a bishop near the king, imagining a pawn capture that opens a file. The defender may instead move the king, exchange queens, or give check. Your line must survive those alternatives to be sound.|What is a useful self-question after finding a promising attack?|If I were defending, what would I try to spoil this? Examine moves that change the nature of the position, not only the reply you hope to see.|Cooperate with your opponent in imagination by trying to defeat your own idea.
Stop at a stable position|Calculation should reach a point where the immediate forcing moves have settled enough for evaluation. Stopping after winning a queen is premature if your king is mated next. Stopping after a sacrifice is equally misleading if the forced recovery has not occurred.|A sequence wins a rook but leaves your queen attacked with check. Continue through that check and any forced recapture before counting the gain. The first visually satisfying moment is not necessarily the endpoint.|What makes an endpoint reasonably stable?|There is no unresolved check, obvious forced capture, or immediate decisive threat that changes the assessment. Quiet positions can still require deeper calculation, but tactical noise must first be resolved.|Evaluate after the forcing dust settles.
Use a blunder check on the future board|A final blunder check examines the position after your intended move. Visualize the piece leaving its old square and arriving at its destination. New lines and abandoned defenders are common sources of mistakes.|Moving a knight to attack the queen may uncover a rook attack on your own king. Even if the knight's destination is safe, the move can be illegal or lose material because of what it stopped defending.|Which three questions belong in the final scan?|Can the opponent check, capture something valuable, or create an immediate forcing threat? Include attacks revealed by your move and defenders you have removed.|Inspect what your move exposes, not only what it attacks.
Forks exploit multiple targets|A fork attacks two or more targets at once. The targets must matter, and the opponent must lack a response that saves both or creates something stronger. Knights are famous for forks, but every piece, including pawns and kings, can double-attack.|A knight check that also attacks a rook is often powerful because the king must answer the check. A fork of two protected pawns may win nothing. Count the actual gain after the defender's best reply.|What should you verify after spotting a fork square?|The forking move is legal, the piece cannot simply be captured, and the opponent cannot answer with a stronger check or counterattack. Then calculate which target is won.|A fork is a promise that must survive the reply.
Pins restrict useful movement|A pinned piece stands between an attacker and a more important target. An absolute pin to the king makes exposing that king illegal. A relative pin, such as to a queen, may be broken if the resulting counterplay justifies the loss.|A knight pinned to its king may appear to defend a nearby square but be unable to capture there. A knight pinned to a queen could still jump with check, forcing the opponent to respond before taking the queen.|What is the first distinction to make when you see a pin?|Is the rear target the king or another piece? Then check whether the pinned piece can move along the line, capture the attacker, or create a stronger threat.|A pin changes legal or practical options; identify which.
Skewers reverse the target order|A skewer attacks a valuable piece in front of a second target on the same line. When the front piece moves, the rear target may fall. Kings make especially forcing front targets because check must be answered.|A rook check along a rank may drive the king away and expose a queen behind it. But the defender may block the check, capture the rook, or move the king while creating protection for the rear piece.|What makes a skewer succeed?|A useful alignment, a forcing attack on the front target, and no defense that saves the rear target without unacceptable loss. Trace the full line and calculate alternatives.|Aligned pieces become vulnerable when the front one must move.
Discovered attacks use two pieces|A discovered attack occurs when one piece moves away and reveals an attack by a piece behind it. The moving piece can make its own threat, creating a double attack. The destination of the blocker is often the key to the combination.|A knight moves off a bishop's diagonal, revealing an attack on the queen while the knight attacks a rook. If the knight also gives check, the opponent's choices may become especially restricted.|How do you search for a discovered attack?|Look for your sliders lined up behind your own pieces toward enemy targets. Then find legal moves for the blocker that create checks, captures, or useful threats of their own.|Before moving a blocker, inspect the line it will uncover.
Remove the defender with a purpose|Capturing, deflecting, or overloading a defender can make a protected target vulnerable. First identify the defender's essential job. Then calculate whether removing it really allows the intended gain, including intermediate checks and replacement defenders.|A bishop guards a mating square and a rook. Forcing it to recapture elsewhere may abandon the mating square. If another piece can take over the defense, the apparent removal may accomplish nothing.|What should you name before sacrificing to remove a defender?|The exact target that becomes vulnerable and the move that exploits it. A sacrifice without a follow-up is merely a material loss unless compensation is demonstrated.|Target the defender's job, then prove the follow-up.
Intermediate moves interrupt routine recaptures|An intermediate move, or zwischenzug, creates a stronger immediate threat before making an expected recapture. Checks are common examples, but attacks on a queen or mate threats can also work. The original recapture must remain available or be replaced by a greater gain.|After your bishop is captured, you may have a checking rook move that forces the enemy king onto a fork square. Recapturing immediately would miss that opportunity. The check must not allow the opponent to save both targets.|What should you scan before any automatic recapture?|Checks, attacks on the queen, mating threats, and captures with tempo. Then compare their consequences with the straightforward recapture.|Treat recapture as a candidate rather than a reflex.
Quiet moves can make forcing threats|A quiet move is neither a check nor a capture, yet it can decide a tactical position by threatening mate, restricting escape squares, or improving coordination. Find it by identifying what your forcing lines are missing.|Your queen is near the king but every check lets the king escape. A quiet bishop move may cover the escape square and threaten an unavoidable mate. The move only works if it gives the opponent no stronger counterplay.|How can failed checking lines help you find a quiet move?|Identify the defender's recurring escape or defensive resource. Look for a move that removes that resource while maintaining your threats. Then test all forcing replies.|Use failed checks to diagnose the missing support.
Defend by changing the attack's conditions|Defense can capture an attacker, block a line, exchange queens, create an escape square, or launch a forcing counterattack. Choose the method that addresses the actual threat. Passive protection is only one option.|If two enemy pieces coordinate against your king, exchanging one may end the attack even at a small material cost. If you merely add a defender, the opponent may remove it and continue the same combination.|What should you identify before choosing a defensive move?|The threatened move and the pieces or lines it requires. Look for a way to disrupt one essential condition, then verify the opponent cannot switch to an equally strong threat.|Break the mechanism of the attack.
Sacrifices need concrete compensation|A sacrifice gives material for something else: mate, attack, activity, structure, or a later recovery. Separate forcing sacrifices from speculative ones. The more material you give, the more carefully you should test the opponent's defensive resources.|Giving a bishop to open a king's pawn shelter may be sound if your queen and rook can join immediately. If your attacking pieces are still undeveloped, the open file may help the defender more than you.|What should you calculate before a king attack sacrifice?|Acceptance, refusal, queen exchanges, and counterchecks. Count attackers that can actually arrive, not pieces you hope will become active several moves later.|Demand a mechanism for compensation, not just an attacking mood.
Time management starts before time trouble|Use more time when the position changes sharply, a forcing line appears, or a major structural decision is required. Save time on familiar, low-risk moves after a brief check. Spending equally on every move is rarely efficient.|A forced recapture may need only a quick scan for intermediate moves. A pawn break that exposes both kings deserves more calculation. If you burn minutes on routine development, you may lack time for the decisive later choice.|What makes a position critical enough for extra time?|Irreversible pawn changes, king exposure, complex exchanges, and multiple plausible forcing candidates. Check your remaining clock before investing heavily.|Spend thinking time where a wrong decision changes the game most.
Analyze your game before using an engine|Your own analysis reveals what you saw, missed, and misunderstood during the game. Write candidate moves and critical moments first, then use an engine to test them. Otherwise you may collect strong moves without learning why your decisions failed.|After a loss, identify where you first felt uncertain and what you expected the opponent to do. Compare those expectations with the actual reply. The engine then helps distinguish a calculation error from a flawed plan.|What should you record at a critical moment?|Your intended plan, considered candidates, expected reply, and time spent. Add the improved explanation after checking. This makes the lesson about your thinking process, not merely the final blunder.|Use analysis tools to test your reasoning, not replace its diagnosis.
Keep an error log with actionable causes|An error log is useful when it groups mistakes by causes you can train: missed checks, loose pieces, weak pawn breaks, or poor time allocation. Labels such as 'played badly' provide no next action. Keep entries brief and specific.|If three games feature the same missed back-rank idea, practice that pattern and add a flight-square scan to your move habit. If losses come from isolated-pawn endings, review exchanges and king activity in that structure.|What makes an error-log entry actionable?|It identifies the position, the missed resource, the reason you missed it, and one practice task. Revisit the entry after several games to see whether the habit improved.|Turn a mistake into a repeatable training action.
Solve for the whole line|A correct first move may come from pattern recognition or a guess. To train calculation, work out the opponent's best response and the continuation before checking the answer. Mark partially solved lines honestly.|You recognize a knight fork but overlook an intermediate check that prevents winning the queen. The first move alone did not demonstrate understanding. Replaying the full line shows which defensive resource you failed to include.|How should you score a puzzle where the first move was right but the continuation was wrong?|Record it as incomplete, identify the missed reply, and solve it again later from the original position. Keep recognition and full calculation as separate measures.|A solution includes the resistance and the finish.
Space repetition around genuine mistakes|Revisiting difficult material after a delay helps test whether you retained the idea. Repetition is most useful when you explain the mechanism, not when you remember a move by its diagram. Increase the interval after reliable full solutions.|Review a missed fork later that day, again after a few days, and later after a longer gap. On each review, name the targets, check the defender's resources, and calculate the line before revealing the answer.|How can you tell memory from understanding?|Change your explanation: state what would prevent the tactic, identify the critical defender, or compare a tempting alternative. Understanding should survive questions beyond the memorized first move.|Repeat the reasoning, not just the move.
Turn each lesson into a game habit|Study becomes useful when it changes decisions during play. After a lesson, choose one small habit to test in your next game. Reviewing whether you used it makes the learning observable and prevents endless passive reading.|After studying loose pieces, add a brief scan of undefended pieces after every opponent move. Do not try to apply twenty new rules at once. A single dependable habit can prevent several types of tactical error.|What should you write after your next game?|One moment where the habit helped or where you forgot it, plus the consequence. If it was too slow or vague, simplify the habit and test it again.|Finish a lesson with one action you can use at the board.
''')
